\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of tables}} &= \boxed{\frac{\text{{Total number of guests}}}{\text{{People per table}}}} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{47}{5}} \\
&= \boxed{9.4}
\end{align*}

However, since we can't have a fraction of a table, we round this up to the nearest whole number.

2. Since 7 people didn't show up, that's 7 less people to fill the tables. Let's revisit our expression for the number of tables, but now calculate the number of people that should be seated at the tables (assuming all people show up):

\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of people who show up}} &= \boxed{47 - 7} \\
&= \boxed{40} \\
\text{{Number of tables}} &= \boxed{\frac{\text{{Number of people who show up}}}{\text{{People per table}}}} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{40}{5}} \\
&= \boxed{8}
\end{align*}

Since these calculations provide the same answer for the number of tables, we know our answer is correct!

3. To solve the problem using the information about the number of tables that 7 people didn't show up, let's first calculate the number of people who were initially scheduled to be seated at the tables that still have 4 people left:

\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of people initially seated}} &= \boxed{{4 \times \text{{Number of remaining tables}}}} \\
&= \boxed{4 \times 8} \\
&= \boxed{32}
\end{align*}

Now let's calculate the number of people who are now missing at the tables that have 4 people seated:

\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of people initially missing}} &= \boxed{47 - \text{{Number of people initially seated}}} \\
&= \boxed{47 - 32} \\
&= \boxed{15}
\end{align*}

Since 7 people didn't show up, there has to be one table that has three missing people from the 47 invited, and all the other tables have four people seated. Now let's find out the number of remaining tables (assuming all tables will still hold the usual 4 people each):

\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of people who should be seated}} &= \boxed{{5 \times \text{{Number of remaining tables}}}} \\
&= \boxed{5 \times 8} \\
&= \boxed{40}
\end{align*}

Now we can calculate the number of leftover tables after finding out that one table is missing 1 person:

\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of remaining tables}} &= \boxed{\frac{\text{{Number of people who should be seated}} - \text{{Number of people initially missing}}}{{5}}} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{40 - 15}{5}} \\
&= \boxed{8}
\end{align*}

Even though this last calculation could have caused confusion with the other calculations due to the missing people, we still obtained the correct answer of \boxed{8} tables.

